{"format":1,"seq":212,"writes":[{"type":"entry","value":{"model":[{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Really verifying this strictly requires an end-to-end test with the actual NVIDIA model through a real harness, which I'll set up later as a temporary vitest file outside the committed test directories. For now, I should move on to writing the actual implementation.\n\nI'm adding a `digestTypeScriptCall` helper to present.ts that summarizes a collapsed call into a title, injected function names, and an outcome string based on the result (error, question asked, saved, or plain success). I'm also refactoring the injected-functions logic into a reusable `injectedIds` helper and planning a generic outcome helper for non-TypeScript tools that just checks error vs. success from the result text.\n\nNow I'm sketching the line-builder function in edits.ts, which assembles the collapsed-call note shown to the user — including byte/char limits, a `CollapsedCall` interface, and the formatting logic for both single and multi-call cases, showing tool name, title, injected functions, outcome, token estimate, and original call IDs. I'm still working out the exact punctuation and joining scheme for multiple collapsed calls to keep it readable but compact.\n\nI'm now designing the truncation algorithm: building the trailing segment with token counts and original call references, falling back to a more compact \"originals\" phrasing if the verbose form is too long, then progressively shortening the description (dropping function names, then truncating with an ellipsis) until everything fits within the byte budget, while also defining the different header prefixes for single versus multiple collapsed calls.\n\nI'm also working out formatting for descriptions across single and multi-call cases, and handling the edge case where IDs alone exceed the budget—in that scenario the description gets dropped entirely while IDs are always preserved, plus figuring out how to safely quote labels that might contain special characters.\n\nNow I'm moving to the collapse planning logic, which decides whether context should be collapsed based on a token threshold, then groups context items into runs to figure out what can be trimmed...\n\nI'm catching an edge case: if a compaction summary's id is newer than the current run's starting id, breaking the loop immediately would wrongly prevent any collapsing, so I need to use `continue` instead to skip over it rather than stop the scan entirely.\n\nI'm figuring out the edit provenance structure for a collapse operation, then writing a helper that replaces an assistant message's content with a summary note while stripping tool calls, and checking that the replacement is actually smaller in tokens than the original plus its results before accepting the edit.\n\nFor each tool call in the message, I need to find its matching result and build a digest, special-casing the \"typescript\" tool to use a dedicated digest function that leverages the present.ts dependency-analysis utilities already included in the tool's durable context entry.\n\nLooking at view.ts, I'm checking how provenance states like \"elide\" or \"collapse\" get rendered as \"elided\" in the visible outline text. For index.ts, I'm defining a collapse option with configurable keepRuns/minTokens (or false to disable), wiring it into the edit-hook creation, and adding a durable guideline note when collapse is active -- while making sure the hook still schedules its task correctly even when there's nothing pending, without adding meaningful overhead to tests.\n\nSince collapse defaults on but small test contexts stay under the 32K floor, existing harness tests won't be affected. Now I'm sketching out a new test suite for collapsing finished runs: adding an options parameter to the host helper, then writing a three-run test where the first two runs get collapsed into short \"[Pit: collapsed typescript call ...]\" summary lines while the third run's tool call remains fully visible with its arguments intact.\n\nI'm also planning a verification test that calls outline and inspectEntry in a fourth run to confirm the collapsed entries still resolve back to their original content by parsing the IDs embedded in the collapsed preview line.\n\nI also want to check the pit.context-edit durable entries to confirm run 3 recorded a collapse operation while run 2 recorded none, since keepRuns was 1.\n\nI should verify idempotence: each collapsed call target should be covered by exactly one collapse operation across all the edit entries, so a fourth run collapsing run 3 doesn't re-touch runs 1-2.\n\nI want to probe the boundary case too: if I change the offset from keepRuns-1 to keepRuns, the test should fail since run 2 would stay uncollapsed after the third run with keepRuns=1. I should parameterize tests across keepRuns values of 1 and 2, and also check that minTokens set extremely high or collapse disabled results in no collapsing at all.\n\nNow I need to figure out how to simulate a failed run in the harness—maybe by exhausting faux responses or triggering an error status—so I can verify that a failed or aborted run drops its staged edits rather than collapsing them. I should check harness.test for any existing \"drops\" test pattern before writing a new one.\n\nFor the elision scenario, I'm realizing keepRuns needs to be set carefully so run 1 isn't already collapsed before the model tries to elide it—using keepRuns:2 means nothing collapses after run 2, leaving run 1 available for the model to elide in run 3.\n\nMaybe I need run 1 to have two separate tool calls, so the model can elide one result while collapse handles the other, producing a single edit entry with both operations in run 3.\n\nI should also set up a fork scenario: forking after run 2, then having run 3 in the parent branch trigger a collapse to see how fork state diverges.\n\nFor the planning tests in context-planning.test.ts, I want to exercise real pairing logic by building synthetic entries — a user message, an assistant call with args, a result, a final assistant text, and another user message — then running planCollapse against that view and checking the note text in the edits. I'll construct a table of row variants (labelled calls, unlabelled calls, failed calls, non-typescript tools, calls with multiple results) each with expected line regexes to pin down formatting boundaries precisely.\n\nI'll need custom args rather than relying on the harness's default placeholder code string, so I'll write my own small example for the typescript call test. For the failed-run case, I should check how harness.test handles \"fails\"/\"aborted\" states to mirror that pattern.\n\n","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_bdrk_018THemkxFpUZuhL4VGkHrBp","name":"typescript","arguments":{"code":"// @pit: {\"label\": \"Find existing failed-run and staged-edit drop tests in durable tests\"}\nasync ({ shell: { exec } }) => {\n  const r = await exec(`grep -n \"fail\\\\|abort\\\\|drops\\\\|pending\" test/durable/harness.test.ts test/durable/questions.test.ts | head -30; grep -n \"getFunctionDependencies\" -r src/functions/dependencies.ts | head -3; sed -n 1,30p src/tool/metadata.ts`);\n  return r.stdout;\n}"}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"nvidia","model":"aws/anthropic/bedrock-claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":5673,"cacheRead":92856,"cacheWrite":4736,"totalTokens":103267,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0,"totalFromProvider":false},"cacheWrite1h":0,"reasoning":5446},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1794904621357,"responseId":"msg_bdrk_ac4ge74nzz4j2q3wwadhscuzgf537d46awtmisgdtj6bfddpjmrq","rawStopReason":"tool_use","durationMs":60475,"thinkingLevel":"medium"}],"kind":"pi.assistant","id":59,"conversationId":1,"byTaskId":58}},{"type":"task","value":{"id":58,"conversationId":1,"kind":"pi.generation","version":2,"input":{},"background":false,"abortReques